## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a beginner-friendly Azure data pipeline that handles schema drift, produces a dimensional model, and captures lineage. It also probes how to isolate raw data, apply transformations, and enforce simple data quality gates with observable provenance.

## Key Concepts
- Schema drift handling in Parquet
- Dimensional modeling (DimUsers, FactEvents)
- Drift capture via JSON blob
- Data quality checks (NOT NULL, timestamp parse)
- Data lineage with Purview integration

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: drift handling in a dataflow step
function transform(record) {
  const known = { user_id, event_time, event_type };
  const drift = _.omit(record, Object.keys(known));
  return { ...record, drift_json: JSON.stringify(drift) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift handling with evolving schemas?
- How would you extend this to handle very high throughput volumes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical use of data ingestion, governance, masking, and a simple warehouse, plus Purview lineage.

## Key Concepts
- Ingestion from multiple sources into ADLS Gen2
- Schema inference and drift handling via Data Flows
- PII masking strategies in pipelines
- Data catalog and lineage in Purview
- Star schema in Synapse

## Code Example
```javascript
function maskPII(value, type){
  switch(type){
    case 'email': return value.replace(/[^@]+@/, '***@');
    case 'ssn': return value.replace(/\d{3}-\d{2}-\d{4}/, 'XXX-XX-XXXX');
    default: return 'MASKED';
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test masking rules across schemas?
- How would you validate lineage completeness in Purview?

## Why This Is Asked

Probes ability to implement a practical, end-to-end beginner pipeline with drift checks and lineage.

## Key Concepts

- Data Factory pipelines and Data Flows
- Drift validation against expected schema
- Spark MERGE upserts in Synapse
- Purview lineage tagging
- Checkpointing and late arrival handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo drift check
function hasDrift(actualSchema, expected) {
  const a = new Set(actualSchema);
  const b = new Set(expected);
  return a.size !== b.size || [...a].some(col => !b.has(col));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle reprocessing of failed files without duplicating rows?
- How would you validate drift in a streaming window vs daily batch?
